{"kind": "plan", "reply": "You could, and it would finish the card faster. Putting all ₱9,050.00 a month on the card pays it off in 8 months, not 16 months. Interest drops to ₱11,257.28, down from ₱21,753.62. That's what the avalanche order you chose points to, because the card's 3% a month is your most expensive debt.

The catch is that the Emergency fund would get nothing. It would stay at 2.2 months of cover, below your 3-month target, and it would never reach that target. Twice already you chose to keep more cash on hand: you wanted a bigger buffer, and when Lia started school you chose to split the money.

So I'd lean towards keeping the split you have now. That's ₱4,000.00 a month to the card on top of its minimum and ₱5,050.00 to the fund. The fund reaches its target in 11 months, and the card is still paid off in 16 months. These are what-ifs only, and nothing has been changed. If your reasons for keeping cash no longer hold, option A is the faster way to clear the card.", "sources": ["figure:debt1-W1:debt.payoff_months@debt:credit-card", "figure:debt.payoff_months@debt:credit-card", "figure:debt1-W1:debt.total_interest@debt:credit-card", "figure:debt.total_interest@debt:credit-card", "figure:savings.emergency_runway_months", "figure:savings.months_to_target", "fact:household.emergency_months_target", "fact:preference.debt_strategy", "fact:debt:credit-card/debt.monthly_rate", "fact:account:emergency-fund/plan.allocation", "fact:debt:credit-card/plan.allocation", "decision:D1", "decision:D2"], "options": [{"label": "A: everything on the card", "what_if": "debt1-W1", "summary": "₱9,050.00 a month on the card clears it in 8 months with ₱11,257.28 interest; the fund gets nothing and stays at 2.2 months.", "sources": ["figure:debt1-W1:debt.payoff_months@debt:credit-card", "figure:debt1-W1:debt.total_interest@debt:credit-card", "fact:preference.debt_strategy", "said"], "specialists": ["debt"]}, {"label": "B: keep the split", "what_if": "savings1-W2", "summary": "₱4,000.00 to the card and ₱5,050.00 to the fund: card paid off in 16 months, fund at target in 11 months.", "sources": ["figure:savings.months_to_target", "figure:savings1-W2:plan.unallocated", "decision:D2", "decision:D1"], "specialists": ["savings", "debt"]}], "recommended": "B: keep the split", "resolution": {"what_if": "savings1-W2", "rejected": "debt1-W1", "reason": "You chose to split the money because Lia is starting school and you want cash on hand. Before that, you chose a bigger cash buffer over paying the car loan early. Keeping the split fits the surplus and gets the fund to its target in 11 months, while the card is still paid off in 16 months."}, "why": "", "topic": "paying down the credit card", "asks": ""}
